///| cmd/sshconfig_kat -- Loop #57: MoonBit's side of the ssh_config cross-check.
///
///|
///
/// Prints the **resolved** options for each `(fixture, host)` pair in
/// `verify_c/sshconfig/cases.txt`, as `key = value` lines under a `CASE` header.
/// The other side of the comparison is OpenSSH itself: `ssh -G -F <fixture> <host>`.
///
///|
///
/// **Why the verdict and not just the parse.** Loop #24 made `parse_ssh_config`
/// and `match_host_option` and stopped there. `match_host_option` was wrong, and
/// wrong in the way that only shows up against a real resolver: it searched the
/// **first** matching `Host` block and reported "no such option" for anything a
/// later block set. The idiom it breaks is the single most common thing in every
/// `~/.ssh/config` on earth:
///
/// ```
/// Host web1
///   User deploy
/// Host *
///   Port 2222
/// ```
///
/// `ssh -G web1` gives `user deploy` and `port 2222`. The old code gave `deploy`
/// and **nothing at all** for Port. It was never noticed because nothing used it.
/// So this program prints what the *rules* resolve to, which is the thing that can
/// disagree with OpenSSH -- a byte-identical parse would not have.
///
///|
///
/// Multi-valued keywords are joined with `|`. `IdentityFile` accumulates across
/// matching blocks in OpenSSH (verified: two `Host x` blocks both survive), while
/// single-valued keywords keep the first. Printing both under one shape means the
/// gate compares order and multiplicity too, not just set membership.
///
/// native only: it reads files, which needs `moonbitlang/async/fs`.
import {
  "moonbitlang/async",
  "moonbitlang/async/fs",
  "openssh/openssh_moonbit/src/sshconfig",
}

options(
  targets: { "main.mbt": [ "native" ] },
)

pkgtype(kind: "executable")